runsc: list bind-mounted RDMA devices as devices under --rdmaproxy

What: with --rdmaproxy, runsc create, run and restore move each mount of the
bundle's spec whose destination starts with /dev/infiniband/uverbs and whose
source is a character device on the host to the spec's devices
(linux.devices) and drop the mount. The device takes the mount's destination
as its path and the host device's major and minor numbers, as an engine that
lists it would, and mode 0666 and root as its owner, as runsc spec gives TPU
devices. They do it where they read the bundle's config.json
(specutils.ReadSpec): the gofer and the sandbox get the spec they read
through a sealed copy (specutils.CopySpec), and later commands from the
container's saved state, so all of them see the devices and none the mounts.
Without --rdmaproxy, or with it set only by a flag annotation, which runsc
applies after it reads the spec, the spec is left as it is.

Why: rdmaproxy proxies only the uverbs devices in linux.devices:
specutils.RDMAEnabled turns RDMA on only for them, the gofer bind-mounts only
them into its /dev (sandboxsetup.SetupDev), and the Sentry registers only
them (boot's rdmaproxyRegisterDevices). Rootless Podman cannot create device
nodes, so for --device /dev/infiniband/uverbsN it bind-mounts the host
device and leaves it out of linux.devices. Under runsc with --rdmaproxy the
sandbox then had no RDMA device, and libibverbs in it failed with "Failed to
get IB devices list: Function not implemented".

Verified:
- //runsc/specutils:specutils_test passes with the new tests of the
  conversion (TestUverbsMountsToDevices, TestReadSpecUverbsMounts).
- On OpenRSI's H100 Slurm nodes, under rootless Podman 4.9.3 with
  --rdmaproxy: Orbit's runsc 0.1.5, which carries this patch, gave a
  Sandbox of two nodes its InfiniBand devices, and NCCL all-reduced 1 GiB
  across their 16 H100s with GPUDirect RDMA at 412 GB/s. Before it, the
  same conversion done by a wrapper that rewrote the bundle's config.json
  before runsc create gave ib_write_bw 391.8 Gb/s between two HCAs of one
  node in gVisor against 393.3 Gb/s on the bare node; without it, no RDMA
  device.

